{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>and after it, the like shall not be</b> The verse informs us, by way of prophecy, that after it the like shall not be. Rashi wrote: that one which was in the days of Joel, concerning which it is said, \"the like of it has not been from of old\" (Joel 2:2) — this teaches us that it was greater than that of Moses. That of Joel consisted of many species, for it combined ארבה and חסיל and ילק and גזם, but that of Moses was of only one species; and the like of it never was and never shall be. And the statement of the Rav is difficult for me, for it is written, \"and He gave their increase to the חסיל, and their labor to the ארבה\" (Psalms 78:46), and it is written, \"He spoke, and the ארבה came, and ילק, without number\" (Psalms 105:34). Perhaps the Rav would say that the species of ארבה of Moses was greater than that of Joel, and the other species of Joel were greater than those of Moses. And these words are null.<br>But <b>throughout all the border of Egypt</b> it continues: <b>before it there was no such locust as it, and after it there shall not be the like</b> there. And it is possible that because the land of Egypt is very moist from the Nile, there would not be a great locust-plague there, for its way is to come in years of withheld rain, as it is mentioned in Joel (Joel 1:20). And Rabbeinu Ḥananel wrote in his Torah commentary: from the time our master Moses prayed until now, no locust causes damage in all of Egypt, and if one falls in the land of Israel and comes and enters the border of Egypt, it does not eat of any produce of the land at all, to this day. And they say that this is already well known to all. Come and see: concerning the frogs it says (Exodus 8:5), \"only in the Nile shall they remain,\" and therefore the crocodile has remained until now; but concerning the locust it is written, \"not one locust remained in all the border of Egypt,\" and upon this it is said (Psalms 105:2), \"speak of all His wonders\" — up to here is the language of the Rav.<br>And my view, according to the plain sense of Scripture, is that because the plague of locusts is accustomed to come at times in all generations, and moreover this one came in the manner of chance occurrences, for an east wind carried it — because of this, Scripture states that it was exceedingly great beyond all that had ever occurred in the chance events of the world, for before it and after it nothing like it comes; and by its greatness they knew that it was a plague from Elohim, for it is not the way of things to come thus at any season, and even that of Joel too was a plague from Elohim."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The reference to Joel 1:20 appears to be Ramban's shorthand for Joel's description of locusts in a drought context; the verse numbering follows the Hebrew Bible. The term אלתמס\"ח in Rabbeinu Ḥananel's citation is Arabic (al-timsāḥ, 'crocodile') and is transliterated as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:50.319633173Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}